<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 267 0 R
/T (FROST: Flexible Round-Optimized Schnorr Threshold signatures)
/E (FROST: Flexible Round-Optimized Schnorr Threshold signatures)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [0]
>>
endobj
552 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1097 0 R
/E (FROST is a cryptographic protocol that allows a defined threshold of participants to collaboratively generate a single, standard Schnorr digital signature. It is backwards compatible with any Schnorr digital signature verifier.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
553 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1098 0 R
/E (It significantly improves digital asset security and privacy by replacing bulky, transparent multi-signature setups with a single aggregate signature that hides the underlying signers and eliminates data bloat.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
554 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1099 0 R
/E (FROST explicitly sacrifices internal fault-tolerance \(robustness\) to achieve unparalleled network speed and unlimited concurrent signing. Instead of relying on computationally heavy error-correction, the protocol uses an "optimistic" approach. if a node fails or acts maliciously, the session immediately aborts, identifies the bad actor, and allows the system to instantly retry.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
555 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1100 0 R
/E (The protocol requires a maximum of two interactive network rounds to complete a signature. By utilizing a batched preprocessing phase to handle nonce generation ahead of time, this can be further condensed into a single, lightning-fast asynchronous round.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
556 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 1101 0 R
/E ( Standardised as RFC 9591 for curves Ed25519, Ed448, P-256, Ristretto255, and secp256k1 as of 2024.)
/Pg 37 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
557 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
